                    <history>
                        <origin><origDate calendar="#Gregorian" when="1270">1270</origDate></origin>
                        <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_1">Exeter, Devon,
                            Cathedral church of St Peter</orgName> <p>This attribution was rejected
                            in the second edition of Medieval Libraries of Great Britain (1964),
                            which did not change in the Supplement (1987). The corresponding
                            Medieval Libraries of Great Britain index card records the following
                            information, which was deemed unsatisfactory for an attribution: On
                            first leaf of text at top: <q>[symbol] Iste liber est nicholai horsley
                            (.......dissono) episcopo Exon' [symbol]</q> (s. xvi; Horsley's name
                            over erasure). Belonged to Bishop Grandison: <q>Liber J. de
                            Grandissono</q> (on verso of last leaf of quire 10). Described in
                            Kerslake's catalogue, <title>Part the first catalogue of literary
                            curiosities</title> [1858], no. 3380.</p></provenance>
                        <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">At foot of first leaf of text a
                            flourish and 'Foxton' written in a minute hand on thin parchment, like a
                            s. xiii bible.</provenance>
                        <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Given to Exeter by <persName>Mr Thomas
                            Kerslake</persName> (the bookseller) of Bristol and Clevedon, February
                            1889.</provenance>
                    </history>
